---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import TypingTutor from '../components/TypingTutor.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { getPage } from '../lib/routes.js';

const page = getPage('/hindi-typing-test');

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is this Hindi typing tutor free?',
    a: 'Yes. No signup, no limit on attempts, no paid tier. It is a static page that runs in your browser, so there is nothing to charge for and nothing to log in to.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is Remington or Kruti Dev typing?',
    a: 'It is the layout most Hindi typing exams still test on. Kruti Dev is a font whose Hindi letters sit on the ordinary English key positions - press <code>d</code> and the font draws <span lang="hi">क</span>. That arrangement came from the Remington typewriter and is what CPCT, SSC, high court and district court, and many state recruitment papers set their Hindi typing test in. You type with an ordinary English keyboard; no Hindi keypad is involved.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Remington or InScript - which one does my exam use?',
    a: 'Check the notification; it names the layout or the font. If it says <strong>Kruti Dev</strong>, <strong>DevLys</strong>, <strong>Remington</strong> or <strong>Remington GAIL</strong>, this tutor is the right one. If it says <strong>InScript</strong> or <strong>Mangal</strong>, that is the Unicode layout built into Windows and you should practise with the Hindi keypad turned on instead. They are different keyboards and practising the wrong one does not carry over.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is the passage in Hindi when I am pressing English keys?',
    a: 'Because that is exactly what Remington typing is. The passage is shown in ordinary Unicode Hindi so it renders on any machine, and underneath it the tutor knows the Kruti Dev keystrokes that produce it - those are what it checks, and what it highlights on the keyboard. It is the same thing that happens in Word when you apply the Kruti Dev font to text you typed on an English keyboard.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do the lessons work?',
    a: 'Eleven levels. The first five open one more row of the keyboard each - home row, top row, bottom row, numbers, Shift. The rest teach what is left once every key is reachable: the matras, the two-key letters like भ and ख, the four conjuncts क्ष त्र ज्ञ श्र, then everyday words, sentences and finally paragraphs. Type the whole drill at or above the level’s accuracy target and the next one unlocks. The gate is accuracy alone and never speed - gating on speed teaches people to rush a drill they have not learned, which is how typists build a plateau they cannot get out of.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does the finger guide show?',
    a: 'The key you must press next is highlighted on the on-screen keyboard, and the finger that should press it is coloured on the two hands below. When a letter needs Shift, the tutor also marks the little finger of the <em>other</em> hand - reaching for the Shift under your typing hand is the single most common habit that caps a typist’s speed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I stop a test early and still see my result?',
    a: 'Yes. <strong>Submit and see result</strong> ends the attempt whenever you press it and shows the full result card: net and gross WPM, accuracy, mistake count and the keys you missed most. It works in both lessons and practice tests, so you can submit at any point rather than waiting for the clock. Two things an early submit does not do: it does not clear a lesson, because you have not typed the whole drill, and it is not saved as a best speed unless you finished the passage, since a flattering number from four words would be one no full-length run could beat.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I pause a test?',
    a: 'Yes. <strong>Pause</strong> stops the clock and freezes every figure where it stands; <strong>Resume</strong> carries on from exactly that point, so the time you were away is not counted against your speed. The Escape key does the same thing, because on a two-hour test your hands are on the keyboard and reaching for a button is the last thing you want. While a test is paused the typing box is switched off - a stopped clock that still accepted keystrokes would be a way to type the passage for free.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long are the tests?',
    a: 'Practice tests run for 1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 15 or 30 minutes, 1 hour or 2 hours, and there is enough queued text that you cannot run out even at 60 words per minute. The long options are there because departmental and court typing papers run far past the ten minutes a speed test needs, and stamina is a separate skill from speed - a typist who is comfortable for one minute is often finished by minute eight. A test that long does reuse paragraphs, since the passage pool is finite. Lessons have no clock at all: the timer counts up and the drill ends when you have typed it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is WPM calculated?',
    a: 'Five keystrokes count as one word, which is the convention every typing test uses and the only fair way to compare a Devanagari passage with a Latin one. The headline number is <strong>net WPM</strong> - correct keystrokes only. The result card also shows gross WPM, which counts every keystroke whether it was right or not. Exams score net.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What typing speed do I need for CPCT or SSC?',
    a: 'It varies by exam and by year, so check the current notification rather than trusting any website. As a rough target, Hindi typing requirements usually sit around 25 to 30 words per minute, and accuracy is normally weighted heavily - a fast run full of mistakes scores worse than a steady clean one. Practise for consistency and the speed follows.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need the Kruti Dev font installed?',
    a: 'No. The passage is Unicode Hindi and renders with the font this site already serves, so nothing has to be installed to practise. You only need Kruti Dev installed to <em>use</em> the output in Word - and if that is what you are after, the <a href="/unicode-to-kruti-dev">Unicode to Kruti Dev converter</a> is the page you want.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is my typing sent anywhere?',
    a: 'No. The passage, the clock, your score and your lesson progress all stay in this browser tab. Nothing is uploaded, there is no account, and no result is recorded anywhere we can see. Progress is kept in your own browser storage on this device and goes away if you clear your browsing data.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I practise on a phone?',
    a: 'You can, but Remington is a ten-finger layout and a phone has a low ceiling for it. The keyboard diagram scrolls to keep the next key in view, so the tutor is usable on a small screen - but a physical keyboard is what this is for.',
  },
];
---

<Base title={page.title} description={page.description}>
  <div class="wrap py-8 sm:py-12">
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl">{page.h1}</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-base text-body">
      Learn Hindi typing on the Kruti Dev Remington layout the exams actually
      use. An on-screen keyboard shows the next key, the hand guide shows which
      finger presses it, and eleven lessons take you from the home row to full
      paragraphs. Practice tests run from 1 minute to 2 hours, you can pause the
      clock, and you can submit at any point to see your result. Free, unlimited,
      and nothing is uploaded.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6">
      <TypingTutor />
    </div>

    <div class="prose-page mt-12 text-body">
      <h2>A tutor, not just a test</h2>
      <p>
        Most Hindi typing pages hand you a paragraph and a clock. That measures
        where you are; it does not move you. This one teaches the layout: the
        key you need next is lit on the keyboard, the finger that should press
        it is coloured on the hands, and the passage marks every akshara green
        or red as you go. When a letter needs Shift, it also shows which little
        finger should be holding it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The passage is ordinary Unicode Hindi, so it renders on any machine with
        nothing installed. What the tutor checks underneath is the Kruti Dev
        keystrokes that produce it, worked out by the same conversion engine
        that powers the converters on this site - so what you read and what you
        press are guaranteed to be the same sentence.
      </p>

      <h2>Remington or InScript</h2>
      <p>
        This is the first thing to get right, because they are different
        keyboards and practising the wrong one is wasted effort.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Remington (Kruti Dev, DevLys)</strong> puts Hindi letters on
          the English key positions. CPCT, SSC, high court and district court
          recruitment and many state posts still set their Hindi typing paper
          this way. If your notification names a font, it is this one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>InScript (Mangal, Unicode)</strong> is the layout built into
          Windows, used for modern office work, portals and anything that ends
          up on a website. It has its own key arrangement and its own muscle
          memory.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This tutor teaches Remington. If you need InScript, turn on the Windows
        Hindi keypad and practise with that instead - and if you only need to
        write a line of Hindi rather than build speed, the
        <a href="/hindi-keyboard">on-screen Hindi keyboard</a> or
        <a href="/english-to-hindi-typing">English to Hindi typing</a> will get
        you there faster.
      </p>

      <h2>The lessons</h2>
      <p>
        Eleven levels. The first five each open one more row. Level 1 is the
        home row alone - <span lang="hi">क</span>, <span lang="hi">स</span>,
        <span lang="hi">र</span>, <span lang="hi">ह</span>,
        <span lang="hi">य</span> and four matras, all typed without moving a
        finger off its resting key. Then the top row, the bottom row, numbers,
        and Shift.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once every key is reachable the levels stop being about rows and start
        being about the script. Level 6 is the matras -
        <span lang="hi">ि ी ु ू े ै ो ौ</span> - on consonants you already know,
        so the only new thing on screen is the vowel sign and the rule that
        <span lang="hi">ि</span> is typed <em>before</em> its letter. Level 7
        drills the letters with no key of their own -
        <span lang="hi">भ</span>, <span lang="hi">श</span>,
        <span lang="hi">ष</span>, <span lang="hi">ण</span>,
        <span lang="hi">ध</span>, <span lang="hi">ख</span>,
        <span lang="hi">घ</span>, <span lang="hi">झ</span>,
        <span lang="hi">थ</span> - which are two keystrokes each and are where
        most Kruti Dev typists quietly lose their speed. Level 8 is the four
        conjuncts <span lang="hi">क्ष</span>, <span lang="hi">त्र</span>,
        <span lang="hi">ज्ञ</span> and <span lang="hi">श्र</span>, which look
        like new letters and are not: they are built from letters you have, with
        the halant you already use. Levels 9 to 11 are everyday words, sentences
        and finally paragraphs.
      </p>
      <p>
        A level unlocks the next when you type the whole drill at its accuracy
        target. Speed is recorded but never required, because gating on speed
        teaches you to rush a drill you have not learned yet.
      </p>

      <h2>Getting faster</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Slow down until you are clean.</strong> Type at whatever pace
          holds you above 95% accuracy, even if it feels absurdly slow. Speed
          follows; corrected habits do not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stop looking at your hands.</strong> Use the on-screen
          keyboard instead - that is what it is for. Every glance down costs you
          your place and resets your rhythm.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Drill the awkward letters on purpose.</strong> The two-key
          consonants and the <span lang="hi">ि</span> matra, which is typed
          <em>before</em> its consonant, are worth more practice than the rest
          of the alphabet combined.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Little and often.</strong> Twenty minutes daily beats three
          hours on a Sunday: muscle memory consolidates between sessions rather
          than during them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sit the full length.</strong> A 30-minute test is a different
          skill from a 1-minute burst, and stamina is the part nobody practises.
          Work up through the lengths rather than always taking the short one -
          the tests here run to two hours for exactly that reason, and the
          clock pauses if you need to stop.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Where this fits with the rest of the site</h2>
      <p>
        If you already have a document in the wrong font, you want a converter
        rather than a tutor: <a href="/">Kruti Dev to Unicode</a>,
        <a href="/unicode-to-kruti-dev">Unicode to Kruti Dev</a>,
        <a href="/devlys-to-unicode">DevLys to Unicode</a>, or the
        <a href="/all-converters">full list</a>. If you want the key chart
        without the lessons, it is on the
        <a href="/hindi-keyboard">Hindi keyboard</a> page.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Faq items={faq} schema={true} />

    <nav aria-labelledby="related" class="mt-12">
      <h2 id="related" class="text-xl">Related tools</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {
          page.related.map((path) => {
            const r = getPage(path);
            return (
              r && (
                <li>
                  <a
                    href={r.path}
                    class="block h-full card p-4 no-underline transition-colors hover:border-saffron"
                  >
                    <span class="block font-medium text-ink">{r.h1}</span>
                    <span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-body">{r.description}</span>
                  </a>
                </li>
              )
            );
          })
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</Base>
